.TH MIRI_EEPROM 1 "October 2026" "libmirisdr-6" "User Commands"
.ad l
.ss 12 0
.SH NAME
miri_eeprom \- read, back up and write the EEPROM of a Mirics based receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B miri_eeprom
.RB [ \-d
.IR index ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Many receivers built on the Mirics MSi2500 keep a small EEPROM next to it. The
chip reads it when it starts, and it can hold the USB ids the chip shows or a
whole firmware image to boot instead of the one in its ROM.
.B miri_eeprom
reads and writes it.
.PP
With the libmirisdr\-6 firmware in the EEPROM the receiver has its own serial
from the moment it is plugged in, so programs find the same receiver again. The
receiver must be unplugged and plugged in again before a new image runs.
.PP
.B Make a backup before anything else.
Writing the firmware replaces what the maker put there, and without a copy it
may be hard to go back to the vendor software. Plug the receiver in again so it
runs from its ROM, and read the EEPROM before any other program opens it:
.PP
.RS
miri_eeprom saferead backup.bin
.RE
.PP
Every command that writes also saves the whole EEPROM first, to
.I miri_eeprom\-backup\-<serial or ids>\-<time>.bin
in the current directory, and writes nothing if that fails.
.B "miri_eeprom write"
with that file puts it back.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI saferead " file" " \fR[\fIbytes\fR] [\fB\-u\fI vid\fB:\fIpid\fR] [\fB\-g\fI reg8\fR]"
The safest way to dump the EEPROM to a file. It does not load the firmware of
the library and does nothing else with the receiver. It counts
.B \-d
among the devices with vendor id 1df7, or with the ids given with
.BR \-u .
.B \-g
sets register 8 of the chip to this value before the read, for boards that need
other pins.
.TP
.B info
Show how the EEPROM is addressed and what its first byte says it holds.
.TP
.B probe
Measure the real size of the EEPROM. What it changes to find out is put back.
.TP
.BI read " file" " \fR[\fIbytes\fR]"
Dump the EEPROM to a file.
.TP
.BI write " file" " \fR[\fIaddress\fR]"
Write a file to the EEPROM, at address 0 unless given. At address 0 the blocks
of a boot image must load below 0x1800.
.TP
.BI writeids " vid pid" " \fR[\fIbcd\fR]"
Have the ROM start with these USB ids.
.TP
.BI writefw " image" " \fR[\fB\-v\fI vid\fR] [\fB\-p\fI pid\fR] [\fB\-s\fI serial\fR]"
Boot this image instead of the ROM.
.B default
is the firmware built into the library. With none of
.BR \-v ,
.B \-p
and
.B \-s
the receiver keeps the ids and serial it has now. Otherwise those not given come
from the image, and
.B \-s """"
gives no serial.
.B "\-s random"
makes one up. The image covers bytes 0x40 to 0x4F, where SDRplay boards keep a
factory string, so only the backup keeps that.
.TP
.B disable
Set the first byte back to 0xFF, so the chip starts from its ROM again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " index"
Which receiver, counting from 0. It may come anywhere on the line.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Also write USB ids the library would not find again, an image it does not know
or boot blocks it would refuse.
.PP
Numbers are decimal unless they start with 0x. The USB ids are hexadecimal, as
.BR lsusb (8)
prints them.
.SH SERIALS OF SDRPLAY BOARDS
The firmware shows up with the USB ids 16d0:158c, which do not tell the board.
The library takes it from the serial instead, so on an SDRplay board the serial
must start with the letter of the board and a dash, followed by the 10
characters of the SDRplay serial:
.B B\-
for the RSP1B,
.B C\-
for the RSPdx\-R2,
.B D\-
for the RSPduo,
.B E\-
for the RSP2 and
.B F\-
for the nRSP\-ST. Other boards take any serial of up to 12 characters.
.SH EXAMPLES
Back up the EEPROM of the second receiver:
.PP
.RS
miri_eeprom \-d 1 saferead backup.bin
.RE
.PP
Put the firmware on an RSP1B, with its SDRplay serial:
.PP
.RS
miri_eeprom writefw default \-s B\-1234567890
.RE
.PP
Go back to the backup:
.PP
.RS
miri_eeprom write backup.bin
.RE
.SH NOTES
After a replug the USB ids change, so udev may need to run its rules again
before a user can open the receiver.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR miri_sdr (1),
.BR miri_fm (1)
.PP
